WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.553
Many properties should never appear in an
ordinary object-update contract:

2
00:00:04.633 --> 00:00:07.875
identifiers, tenant and owner links, roles
and permissions,

3
00:00:07.955 --> 00:00:11.884
administrative flags, account status,
verification and approval state,

4
00:00:11.964 --> 00:00:15.331
billing state, balances, audit fields, and
creation metadata.

5
00:00:15.411 --> 00:00:18.092
They are server-owned relative to that
operation.

6
00:00:18.172 --> 00:00:20.292
This does not mean they can never change;

7
00:00:20.372 --> 00:00:23.614
it means a generic edit route is not
authorized to change them.

8
00:00:23.694 --> 00:00:26.999
Model significant changes as explicit
application commands.

9
00:00:27.079 --> 00:00:30.633
Role assignment, account disabling,
project transfer, approval,

10
00:00:30.713 --> 00:00:35.140
and tenant migration deserve dedicated
service methods or endpoints with their own

11
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.896
authorization, prerequisites, transaction
rules, audit records, and notifications.

12
00:00:39.976 --> 00:00:41.535
The exact HTTP shape can vary.

13
00:00:41.615 --> 00:00:46.042
The security benefit comes from naming the
transition and preventing it from hiding

14
00:00:46.122 --> 00:00:48.055
inside a bag of optional properties.

15
00:00:48.135 --> 00:00:52.313
Give each command its own narrow input
rather than accepting the entity type and

16
00:00:52.393 --> 00:00:54.076
promising to use only one field.

17
00:00:54.156 --> 00:00:57.960
A privileged operation should load the
target under the proper tenant or

18
00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.971
administrative scope, verify the caller's
authority,

19
00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:05.104
validate the requested transition, and
enforce current-state invariants.

20
00:01:05.184 --> 00:01:08.613
Ownership transfer may require acceptance
and conflict checks.

21
00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:11.374
Approval may require a particular workflow
state.

22
00:01:11.454 --> 00:01:15.819
Balance changes may need ledger operations
rather than direct field replacement.

23
00:01:15.899 --> 00:01:19.453
Database constraints and transactions can
reinforce these rules,

24
00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:22.276
but they do not decide who may invoke the
transition.

25
00:01:22.356 --> 00:01:26.534
Avoid blocklists that merely remove
today's sensitive names from a broad input

26
00:01:26.614 --> 00:01:27.051
object.

27
00:01:27.131 --> 00:01:30.124
New fields can bypass a list nobody
remembered to update,

28
00:01:30.204 --> 00:01:34.194
and nested writes can expose equivalent
capabilities under another shape.

29
00:01:34.274 --> 00:01:36.831
Start from an allowlist specific to the
command.

30
00:01:36.911 --> 00:01:40.839
Ordinary property editing and privileged
state transition are different

31
00:01:40.919 --> 00:01:44.660
capabilities, even when both eventually
update the same database row.
